Subject: [PATCH 52/52] the three ways a plain `openbios-unix` session went wrong
Arch-tested: x86 amd64 ppc unix

All three came out of ONE user session that tried to get a prompt and mistyped
the command. The typo was theirs; everything below is ours.

--- 1. A MISSING DICTIONARY WAS NEITHER NAMED NOR FATAL ------------------

    $ openbios-unix ./no-such.dict
    fword: 'is-noname-cfunc' is not in the dictionary -- nothing was executed
    fword: 'PREPOST-initializer' is not in the dictionary -- nothing was executed
    not supported.
    $ echo $?
    0

read_dictionary() returns 0 when stat() fails, and the CALLER IGNORED IT, so the
engine ran on with an empty dictionary and produced two baffling `fword:` lines
about words it had never loaded, then "not supported.", then SUCCESS. The file
that could not be read was never mentioned. It is now, and it is a failure:

    panic: cannot read the dictionary './no-such.dict' -- no such file, or
    not readable.                                                   (exit 1)

--- 2. END OF INPUT SPUN A CORE AT 100% FOREVER -------------------------

kernel/forth.c's `key` was `while (!availchar());` -- a busy-wait NOTHING could
interrupt. availchar() answering "no key yet" is indistinguishable from "there
will never be another key", and at EOF arch/unix answered the first forever.
Measured: state R, no wchan, 100% CPU, indefinitely. Ctrl-D at the prompt hung.
So did any piped script not ending in `bye`.

IT TOOK BOTH HALVES, and the first attempt shipped only one. The console layer
is what knows EOF happened, so it says so (FORTH_INTSTAT_STOP -- the flag
kernel/bootstrap.c's availchar() already sets for the dictionary path); and the
waiter has to LISTEN, which is the one shared line. Fixing the console alone
changed nothing at all, because key() never looked at the flag.

Then EOF must not be echoed AS A BYTE: returning EOF from getchar pushed
(char)-1 into the line editor, which printed a row of 0xff before unwinding. It
returns '\r' now -- end of input reads as end of LINE, which is what the reader
is waiting for and what every REPL does with Ctrl-D. And EOF counts as a
DELIBERATE exit beside `bye` (TODO 18(b)), so the status is 0, not 1.

KNOWN AND NOT HIDDEN: a Ctrl-D exit prints three or four redundant `0 >  ok`
prompts on the way out, because each interpret cycle re-enters enterforth, whose
entry clears interruptforth, so the flag must be re-set. It terminates and it is
honest; making it exit on the first one needs the outer interpreter's own
`terminate?`, which is a larger change than this.

--- 3. EVERY CLEAN EXIT PRINTED A FAILURE -------------------------------

    feval: 0 to terminate? -- threw -4

packages/cmdline.c's comment says "Reset stack and terminate?" and it reset only
the RETURN stack, so this feval ran at a NEGATIVE data-stack depth left by the
unwind and interpreter.fs's `depth 0< if -4 throw` fired. The phrase is fine --
`0 to terminate?` typed at the prompt answers ` ok`. It was the engine
underneath that had been torn down. One line does what the comment already
claimed.

That line was printed by OUR OWN reporting patch (the Forth bindings report
their own failures), which is the patch working: it surfaced a real throw nobody
had seen. But a warning on every healthy run is how real warnings stop being
believed, and the `diagnostics` track already asserts ZERO feval/fword lines on
a clean boot for the QEMU arches. The unix target had no track at all until
today, which is why this survived.

Covered by `smoke-openbios.sh unix`, which now pins all three: a missing
dictionary is named and non-zero; end-of-input exits 0 within a BOUNDED timeout
(a test for a hang that itself hangs tells you nothing) and echoes no 0xff; and
a clean session prints no feval/fword line.

diff --git a/arch/unix/unix.c b/arch/unix/unix.c
index cb9757e..2f09466 100644
--- a/arch/unix/unix.c
+++ b/arch/unix/unix.c
@@ -17,6 +17,7 @@
 #include <termios.h>
 #include <sys/types.h>
 #include <sys/stat.h>
+#include <sys/mman.h>
 #include <stdarg.h>
 
 #ifdef __GLIBC__
@@ -62,6 +63,7 @@ static ucell *memory;
 static int diskemu;
 
 static int segfault = 0;
+static int forth_exit_status = 0;
 static int verbose = 0;
 
 #if defined(CONFIG_PPC) || defined(CONFIG_SPARC64)
@@ -182,6 +184,23 @@ static ucell read_dictionary(char *fil)
  * functions used by primitives
  */
 
+/* LOCAL DIVERGENCE (mklab). EOF IS AN EXIT, NOT "NOTHING YET".
+ *
+ * This returned 0 at EOF -- the same answer as "no key has been typed" -- so
+ * the Forth key loop spun on it forever. Measured: 100% CPU, state R, no wchan,
+ * indefinitely. Ctrl-D at the prompt, or a piped script that does not end in
+ * `bye`, both hung. It only became reachable when patch 50 let this target get
+ * as far as an input loop; before that it aborted during initialisation.
+ *
+ * kernel/bootstrap.c's availchar() already had the answer for the DICTIONARY
+ * path -- set FORTH_INTSTAT_STOP so the interpreter loop ends -- and the
+ * runtime console path simply had no equivalent. It does now.
+ *
+ * And EOF is DELIBERATE: Ctrl-D is how every REPL is left, so it counts as a
+ * clean exit beside `bye` (TODO 18(b)) rather than as a bootstrap that died.
+ */
+static int saw_eof = 0;
+
 static int unix_availchar(void)
 {
 	int tmp = getc(stdin);
@@ -189,6 +208,8 @@ static int unix_availchar(void)
 		ungetc(tmp, stdin);
 		return -1;
 	}
+	saw_eof = 1;
+	interruptforth |= FORTH_INTSTAT_STOP;
 	return 0;
 }
 
@@ -200,7 +221,20 @@ static int unix_putchar(int c)
 
 static int unix_getchar(void)
 {
-	return getc(stdin);
+	int c = getc(stdin);
+
+	/* END OF INPUT READS AS END OF LINE, not as a byte. Returning EOF here
+	 * pushed (char)-1 into the line editor, which echoed it -- Ctrl-D printed
+	 * a row of 0xff before the interpreter unwound. A carriage return is what
+	 * the reader is waiting for and what every REPL does with Ctrl-D: finish
+	 * the line, then leave. The STOP flag set beside it is what actually ends
+	 * the wait in kernel/forth.c's key(). */
+	if (c == EOF) {
+		saw_eof = 1;
+		interruptforth |= FORTH_INTSTAT_STOP;
+		return '\r';
+	}
+	return c;
 }
 
 static struct _console_ops unix_console_ops = {
@@ -363,13 +397,72 @@ int_handler(int signo __attribute__ ((unused)),
     exit(1);
 }
 
+/* LOCAL DIVERGENCE (mklab). TODO 18.
+ *
+ * PLACE THE FORTH ARENA AND THE DICTIONARY BELOW 4 GiB.
+ *
+ * 1275 encodes an integer into FOUR bytes (5.3.5.1), and /chosen's `stdin` and
+ * `stdout` are ihandles -- pointers into these two allocations
+ * (forth/admin/iocontrol.fs:42,76). At run time pointer2cell is a plain cast
+ * (include/kernel/stack.h:35; Makefile.target defines
+ * NATIVE_BITWIDTH_EQUALS_HOST_BITWIDTH for every target), so an ihandle IS a
+ * host pointer. glibc hands these two sizes out well above 4 GiB on a 64-bit
+ * host, so encode-int refused -- correctly -- and the firmware halted during
+ * initialisation, which is what MANUAL_TESTING.md section 5 recorded.
+ *
+ * THIS DOES NOT MAKE 1275 ABLE TO HOLD A 64-BIT POINTER, and it is not a
+ * weakening of patch 26's gate, which stays exactly as it is. It puts this
+ * target where every other target has always been: the QEMU firmwares live at
+ * 0x400000 and 0x4000000, so their pointers have always fitted in four bytes.
+ *
+ * AND IT REFUSES BY NAME RATHER THAN DRIFTING BACK. If the low mapping cannot
+ * be had, we say so and exit non-zero instead of continuing into an abort that
+ * used to exit 0 (TODO 18(b)) -- an honest failure the caller can see.
+ */
+#define OF_LOW_LIMIT   ((uintptr_t)1 << 32)
+
+static void *alloc_below_4g(size_t len, uintptr_t hint, const char *what)
+{
+	void *p = mmap((void *)hint, len, PROT_READ | PROT_WRITE,
+	               MAP_PRIVATE | MAP_ANONYMOUS, -1, 0);
+
+	if (p == MAP_FAILED)
+		return NULL;
+
+	/* A hint is only a hint: verify, never assume. */
+	if ((uintptr_t)p + len > OF_LOW_LIMIT) {
+		printk("panic: %s landed at %p, above 4 GiB -- an ihandle there "
+		       "cannot be encoded into the four bytes IEEE 1275 gives an "
+		       "integer (5.3.5.1). See TODO 18.\n", what, p);
+		munmap(p, len);
+		return NULL;
+	}
+	return p;
+}
+
+/* The matching free, and it is load-bearing rather than tidiness.
+ *
+ * These two are mmap'd now, so the free()s that used to end main() hand glibc a
+ * pointer it never allocated -- and free() reads its chunk header BELOW the
+ * pointer, at p-8. That read is what produced "panic: segmentation violation at
+ * 1ffffff8" (arena base minus 8) while the page below was unmapped, and
+ * "free(): invalid pointer" once it was mapped and glibc got to inspect the
+ * garbage it found. One bug, two costumes. Measured: with munmap here and NO
+ * guard page, the session runs clean, and a gdb access-watchpoint on
+ * memory-8/-16 never fires across a whole session. */
+static void free_below_4g(void *p, size_t len)
+{
+	if (p)
+		munmap(p, len);
+}
+
 /*
  * allocate memory and prepare engine for memory management.
  */
 
 static void init_memory(void)
 {
-	memory = malloc(MEMORY_SIZE);
+	memory = alloc_below_4g(MEMORY_SIZE, 0x20000000, "the Forth arena");
 	if (!memory) {
 		printk("panic: not enough memory on host system.\n");
 		exit_terminal();
@@ -521,7 +614,9 @@ int main(int argc, char *argv[])
 	/* Initialise console */
 	init_console(unix_console_ops);
 
-	if ((dict = (unsigned char *) malloc(DICTIONARY_SIZE)) == NULL) {
+	if ((dict = (unsigned char *) alloc_below_4g(DICTIONARY_SIZE,
+	                                            0x30000000,
+	                                            "the dictionary")) == NULL) {
 		printk("panic: not enough memory.\n");
 		return 1;
 	}
@@ -556,7 +651,17 @@ int main(int argc, char *argv[])
         if (verbose)
             printk("done.\n");
 
-	read_dictionary(argv[optind]);
+	/* LOCAL DIVERGENCE (mklab). read_dictionary() returns 0 when stat() fails
+	 * and this call used to IGNORE it, so a missing or misspelt dictionary ran
+	 * on with an EMPTY one: two baffling `fword: ... is not in the dictionary`
+	 * lines, "not supported.", and exit 0. The file that could not be read was
+	 * never named. Say which file, and fail. */
+	if (!read_dictionary(argv[optind])) {
+		printk("panic: cannot read the dictionary '%s' -- no such file, "
+		       "or not readable.\n", argv[optind]);
+		exit_terminal();
+		exit(1);
+	}
 	forth_init();
 
 	PUSH_xt( bind_noname_func(arch_init) );
@@ -580,12 +685,50 @@ int main(int argc, char *argv[])
 		}
 
 		enterforth((xt_t)PC);
+
+		/* LOCAL DIVERGENCE (mklab). TODO 18(b): DO NOT REPORT SUCCESS FOR A
+		 * BOOTSTRAP THAT DIED.
+		 *
+		 * main() used to `return 0` here whatever the Forth had done, so a
+		 * firmware that aborted during initialisation -- printing a refusal
+		 * and never reaching its prompt -- told the shell it had succeeded.
+		 * A false success outranks an honest failure, so it is the part
+		 * worth fixing.
+		 *
+		 * THREE OBVIOUS SIGNALS DO NOT WORK, measured, and are recorded here
+		 * so nobody re-derives them: enterforth()'s return is 0 in both cases
+		 * (an uncaught throw unwinds the return stack to the same depth `bye`
+		 * does, so rstackcnt balances); interruptforth's STOP bit is 0 in
+		 * both (it marks input EOF, not how the session ended); and
+		 * kernel/bootstrap.c's exception() serves the dictionary-BUILD path
+		 * and is never called while running a prebuilt dict.
+		 *
+		 * So the flag is set on the one path that is DELIBERATE -- `bye`, in
+		 * forth/bootstrap/start.fs -- and read here through feval(), the
+		 * shipped C-to-Forth call, rather than by reaching into the
+		 * dictionary at a guessed offset. If the word is missing we say
+		 * UNKNOWN and keep the old status: "I could not check" must never
+		 * render as a failure any more than as a pass.
+		 */
+		if (findword("of-left-cleanly")) {
+			feval_quiet("of-left-cleanly @");
+			if (POP() == 0 && !saw_eof) {
+				printk("openbios-unix: the Forth engine was left "
+				       "WITHOUT `bye` -- initialisation did not "
+				       "complete. See TODO 18(b).\n");
+				forth_exit_status = 1;
+			}
+		} else {
+			printk("openbios-unix: UNKNOWN -- `of-left-cleanly` is not "
+			       "in this dictionary, so whether the engine exited "
+			       "deliberately could not be checked (TODO 18(b)).\n");
+		}
 #if 0
 		if (dictname != NULL)
 			write_dictionary(dictname);
 #endif
 
-		free(memory);
+		free_below_4g(memory, MEMORY_SIZE);
 
 	} else {		/* input file is not a dictionary */
 		printk("not supported.\n");
@@ -595,8 +738,8 @@ int main(int argc, char *argv[])
 	if (diskemu!=-1)
 		close(diskemu);
 
-	free(dict);
-	return 0;
+	free_below_4g(dict, DICTIONARY_SIZE);
+	return forth_exit_status;
 }
 
 #undef printk
diff --git a/kernel/forth.c b/kernel/forth.c
index 61dd70d..888b6d5 100644
--- a/kernel/forth.c
+++ b/kernel/forth.c
@@ -1721,7 +1721,22 @@ static void iskey(void)
 
 static void key(void)
 {
-	while (!availchar());
+	/* LOCAL DIVERGENCE (mklab). A BUSY-WAIT THAT NOTHING COULD INTERRUPT.
+	 *
+	 * This was `while (!availchar());` -- and availchar() answering "no key
+	 * yet" is indistinguishable from "there will never be another key". On
+	 * arch/unix at EOF (Ctrl-D, or a piped script not ending in `bye`) that
+	 * spun at 100% CPU forever: measured, state R, no wchan, indefinitely.
+	 *
+	 * The console layer is what knows the difference, and it already has a way
+	 * to say so -- kernel/bootstrap.c's availchar() sets FORTH_INTSTAT_STOP
+	 * when its input is exhausted. This loop simply never looked. Now it does,
+	 * so a console that reaches end-of-input can end the wait instead of
+	 * needing a key that is not coming.
+	 *
+	 * Unchanged where no one sets STOP, which is every QEMU target: there
+	 * availchar() returns 0 only while a key has not arrived YET. */
+	while (!availchar() && !(interruptforth & FORTH_INTSTAT_STOP));
 #ifdef FCOMPILER
 	PUSH(get_inputbyte());
 #else
diff --git a/packages/cmdline.c b/packages/cmdline.c
index ea6bca3..2a8b01e 100644
--- a/packages/cmdline.c
+++ b/packages/cmdline.c
@@ -254,7 +254,9 @@ cmdline_prompt( cmdline_info_t *ci )
 
 			emit_str( &buf[pos] );
 			emit(' ');
-			PUSH( feval(buf) );
+			/* feval_quiet, because print-status on the very next
+			 * line IS the report. See libopenbios/bindings.c. */
+			PUSH( feval_quiet(buf) );
 			fword("print-status");
 
 			/* Leave the interpreter if terminate? value set */
@@ -398,7 +400,16 @@ go_up:
          * e.g. for debugging */
 
 	/* Reset stack and terminate? */
+	/* LOCAL DIVERGENCE (mklab). The comment above says "Reset stack" and only
+	 * the RETURN stack was reset, so this feval ran with a NEGATIVE data-stack
+	 * depth left by the unwind -- interpreter.fs's `depth 0< if -4 throw` fired,
+	 * and every clean exit printed
+	 *     feval: 0 to terminate? -- threw -4
+	 * The phrase is fine; `0 to terminate?` typed at the prompt answers ` ok`.
+	 * It was the engine underneath it that had been torn down. A warning on
+	 * every healthy run is how real warnings stop being believed. */
 	rstackcnt = dbgrstackcnt;
+	dstackcnt = 0;
 	feval("0 to terminate?");
 }
 
